    id: "csv-table",
    name: "CSV to aligned table",
    spec: "Arg 1 is CSV text; print it as an aligned text table. Parsing: first replace every \\r\\n with \\n. If arg 1 is the empty string print exactly \"error: empty\". Records are separated by newlines; one newline at the very end of the text does not start a new record. Fields are separated by commas. A field that starts with a double quote is quoted: it continues to its closing quote, commas and newlines inside it are part of the field, and two double quotes in a row inside it stand for one double quote. Records are numbered from 1 (the header is record 1). Errors, printed exactly and alone, the first one met when reading the text from the start: \"error: bad quote in record <n>\" for a double quote inside an unquoted field, or a closing quote followed by anything other than a comma, a newline or the end of the text; \"error: unterminated quote\" for a quoted field that never closes; \"error: record <n> has <k> fields, expected <m>\" when a record ends with a different number of fields than the header has. Output: in every cell, replace each newline with the two characters backslash and n. A column's width is the largest number of characters (code points) among its header and cells. A column is numeric if at least one of its non-header cells is non-empty and every non-empty non-header cell matches an optional minus sign, one or more digits, and optionally a dot followed by one or more digits. Print a border line, the header row, a border line, every other record as a row, and then a final border line only if there was at least one row after the header. A border line is \"+\" then, for each column, (width + 2) dashes followed by \"+\". A row is \"|\" then, for each column, a space, the cell padded with spaces to the column width, a space and \"|\". Cells (header included) in numeric columns are right-aligned; all others are left-aligned.",
