    spec: "Arg 1 is a program in a small Lisp. Run it and print one line per result as described below. READING: put spaces around every \"(\" and \")\", then split on whitespace into tokens. An atom matching -?[0-9]+ is an integer; #t and #f are the booleans; any other atom is a symbol. A program is a sequence of expressions (an atom or a parenthesized list of expressions). If the parentheses do not balance (an unclosed list, or a \")\" with no open list) print exactly \"error: syntax\" and nothing else, before running anything. VALUES: integers, booleans, lists and functions. EVALUATION: an integer or boolean evaluates to itself; a symbol evaluates to its value in the nearest enclosing environment, else error \"unbound <symbol>\". A list whose first element is one of these symbols is a special form: (define name expr) evaluates expr and binds name in the CURRENT environment; (define (name p1 p2 ...) body1 body2 ...) binds name to a function, the same as (define name (lambda (p1 p2 ...) body1 ...)); (lambda (p1 p2 ...) body1 body2 ...) makes a function that closes over the current environment (lexical scope); calling it with the wrong number of arguments is error \"arity\"; each call gets a new environment whose parent is the closed-over one, binds the parameters, runs the bodies in order and returns the last value; (if c a b) evaluates c, and evaluates and returns b if c is #f and a otherwise (everything except #f counts as true, including 0 and the empty list); (let ((n1 e1) (n2 e2) ...) body1 ...) evaluates every ei in the CURRENT environment, then runs the bodies in a new child environment holding those bindings and returns the last value; (begin e1 e2 ...) evaluates in order and returns the last value. A special form with the wrong shape (define with a missing or extra part, if without exactly 3 parts, lambda or let or begin with no body, a parameter part that is not a list of symbols, a let binding that is not a two-element list starting with a symbol) is error \"syntax\", and so is evaluating the empty list (). Any other list is a call: evaluate its first element, then its arguments left to right, then if the first element's value is not a function it is error \"not a function\". BUILTINS (functions bound in the global environment): + and * take any number of integers (none gives 0 and 1); - with one integer negates it and with more subtracts the rest from the first, left to right (no arguments is \"arity\"); / takes exactly 2 integers and divides, truncating toward zero (dividing by 0 is error \"division by zero\"); = < > take exactly 2 integers and return a boolean; not takes 1 value and returns #t only for #f; list returns a list of its arguments; cons takes a value and a list and returns a new list with the value in front; car and cdr take a non-empty list and return its first element and the rest; null? takes 1 value and returns #t only for the empty list. A builtin given the wrong number of arguments is error \"arity\" (checked first); given a value of the wrong kind (a non-integer for arithmetic or comparison, a non-list for cons's second argument or for car/cdr, or an empty list for car/cdr) it is error \"type\". OUTPUT: evaluate the top-level expressions in order in the global environment; after each one that is not a define form, print its value: integers in decimal, #t / #f, lists as \"(\" + elements separated by single spaces + \")\" (so the empty list is \"()\"), functions as \"<fn>\". On the first error print \"error: <message>\" (for example \"error: unbound foo\"), after any lines already printed, and stop.",
